/-
Fmt/M2ProgLRFiles.lean: test data for a verifier, namely one set of files of the second toy as
bytes: parameters, public file, the proof, its 13 altered copies, and lists of all further files
with what the archive says of each.

The set of files: tiny parameters with a repeated position. These are the tiny parameters under
another key of the prover's coins. Three of the toy's rounds are named LOC, and each draws a list of
positions. At that key the second of the three draws one position twice. That round comes after a
link with a kept message (a message that the next link sends again).

Sources. This file was generated by a program from files in the archive
toy-files-made-by-the-cuts-tools.tgz (sha256
f97d6774317660e9033841ab36ce4e63e376ebeb25542cf6f1f92e8f61644aa1; the archive is not in this
repository), which the programs (the C++ implementation that comes with this Lean code) made for the
second toy (a chain of three links; Fmt/M2Dec.lean says what it is). The data are made-up toy data:
no model and no weights. Taken from the archive:
honest-and-altered/links/tiny-with-a-repeat/tiny-links.parameters, .configuration, .proof,
.altered-01.proof to .altered-13.proof, .altered.tsv. The generating program stops unless every file
that the archive's own list of checksums (SUMS) names, the lists among them, has the sha256 given
there. The file imports no Mathlib. The archive ships the altered copies as files. Each differs from
the proof in a few bytes at one place, or lacks its last byte, or has one byte more. Here each is
defined as that function of the proof. The function was found by the generating program by comparing
bytes, and the program stops unless it gives the shipped file byte for byte.

1. What the file contains.
   params, pubs, proof   The parameter bytes, the list of public files (one), the honest proof.
   f_altered_01 ..       The 13 altered copies.
   table                 Name, bytes and the number of the check that refuses. For an altered copy
                         the number is taken from the words of the programs' own verifier in
                         .altered.tsv, by an exact table of its sentences (the generating program
                         stops at a sentence that it does not know); for a false proof it is what
                         was expected beforehand. 1, the form; 2, proof of work; 3, a Merkle
                         opening; 4, the linear check of the last block of the proximity test; 5,
                         that block's checks at the drawn positions; 0, accepted. The honest proof
                         is first, with 0. This set has no false proofs.
   refusedOnly           Files that must be refused, by whichever check: altered-07, in which the
                         word shown in the last round is changed. The programs' two verifiers make
                         the linear check first (4); a verifier that recomputes the whole hash chain
                         first meets the proof of work (2).
   further               All 13 further files, by name.

2. What is checked when the file compiles (#guard). All sizes. A checksum of every file, the altered
copies as made here included, each against the shipped file's value (not sha256, which this
development does not have). Each byte string is tied to the archive in two steps. Outside Lean, the
reference program of the format page (docs/format/FORMAT.md, which comes with the programs), in the
copy that the archive ships (format_rules.py; that copy belongs to an edition of the page that is
not the one that ships), computed the format's hash with domain number 168 of the file, at the
moment when the file's sha256 had been compared with the archive's list. In Lean, a test compares
that value with the same hash of the bytes in this file (HF, Fmt/Rules5Fast.lean, which is proved
equal to the format's hash). The lists `table` and `refusedOnly` together have as many members as
the list `further`, and one more for the honest proof.

3. Theorems. None.

4. What is not claimed.
   No theorem. The tests (#guard) are evaluations that Lean makes when the file compiles. They speak
   of these bytes and of nothing else.
   Nothing is said here about what any verifier in this development answers on these files; see
   Fmt/M2EngineLR.lean, Fmt/M2EngineLRw*.lean and Run/M2Engine*.lean.
-/
/- Limits: that a proof file is accepted or refused is no theorem of this library. It is the outcome
